  "citations": [
    {
      "role": "cite",
      "name": "habeas:coverage",
      "commentary": "This private compatibility module only declares the legacy resource and\n selector address shapes; it has no handwritten executable code. Its derived\n serialization and identity operations do not emit source-line measurements.\n The provider tests in `sources/tests.rs` exercise resource identities.\n `tests/outline_content.rs` covers the public path-based item addresses."
    }
  ],
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "The `habeas:coverage` citation on the `crates/trust-but-verify/src/resource.rs` root is appropriate, and the commentary agrees with the current source.\n\n- **Source contents:** The file only declares `struct[ResourceId]` and `struct[OutlineId]`. Both carry derives for `Clone`, `Debug`, `PartialEq`, `Eq` and serde, and there is no handwritten function body. This supports the claim that the module has no handwritten executable code and no source-line measurements.\n- **Compatibility and privacy:** `OutlineId` is `#[cfg(test)]`. `lib.rs` declares `mod resource;` without `pub`. That matches the description of a private compatibility module.\n- **Provider tests:** `crates/trust-but-verify/src/sources/tests.rs` imports `ResourceId`. Its `fn[id]` helper constructs `ResourceId` values, and the `Memory` and `FailingProvider` providers use them. So the provider tests do exercise resource identities.\n- **Path-based tests:** `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/outline_content.rs` has tests that build outlines from paths with `OutlineContent::from_source` and select items by selector. For example, `selected_item_reads_checked_file_spans_with_snapshot_redaction` does this. That supports the claim about public path-based item addresses.\n\nThe justification is substantive and the file exception is proposed on a file root, as the built-in permits.",
